Ladybird aligns the last line of a block, and a line before a forced
break, per text-align-last, where auto means text-align, except that
justify becomes start. Blink does the same in ComputedStyle::
GetTextAlign(is_last_line), WebKit in InlineFormattingUtils’s
computedHorizontalAlignment (and InlineLineBuilder for justification),
and Gecko in nsLineLayout::TextAlignLine(), via nsStyleText::
TextAlignForLastLine(). Run in Chrome 157, Firefox 151, and WebKit
26.5, a page of 14 variants (a soft-wrapped block, a line before a br,
a preserved newline, a block child, RTL, a single line, an inline child,
text-justify: none, and match-parent) aligns its lines by the same rule
in all three, and in Ladybird, but for one: match-parent under an RTL
parent’s text-align-last: start. WebKit and Ladybird align that line
right, as the spec says, since start resolves against the parent’s
direction. But Chrome and Firefox align it left, by the child’s start.
